Body:
The repeated ALSA startup path is now fixed in #41770 and the first native diagnostics block is tracked upstream in anomalyco/opentui#1358.

To identify why scrolling the `/` menu coincided with an attention sound request, could you capture only the four built-in notification event families? This intentionally avoids prompts, tool input/output, file contents, and provider payloads.

Create `~/.config/opencode/plugins/attention-timeline.ts`:

```ts
import { appendFile, mkdir } from "node:fs/promises"
import path from "node:path"

export default {
  id: "attention-timeline",
  async tui(api: any) {
    const file = path.join(process.env.HOME ?? "", ".cache/opencode/attention-timeline.log")
    await mkdir(path.dirname(file), { recursive: true })
    const line = (value: string) =>
      appendFile(file, `${new Date().toISOString()} ${value}\n`).catch(() => {})

    for (const type of [
      "question.asked",
      "permission.asked",
      "session.status",
      "session.error",
    ]) {
      api.event.on(type, (event: any) => {
        const sessionID = event.properties.sessionID ?? "-"
        const parentID = api.state.session.get(sessionID)?.parentID ?? "-"
        const status =
          type === "session.status"
            ? event.properties.status?.type ?? "unknown"
            : type
        void line(`${type} session=${sessionID} parent=${parentID} status=${status}`)
      })
    }
  },
}
```

Then restart OpenCode, reproduce the `/` menu scrolling until the ALSA block appears, note its wall-clock time, quit, and share `~/.cache/opencode/attention-timeline.log` plus that timestamp. Please remove the plugin afterward.

This will tell us whether the sound request came from a question, permission, session completion, session error, or from outside the built-in notification path. Please redact session IDs if needed.
